ourceSlug": "rené-de-prie", "sourceName": "René de Prie", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "rome-italy", "targetName": "Rome, Italy", "context": "René de Prie spent significant periods in Rome as a cardinal, participating in the papal curia before and after his period of schism."}, {"sourceSlug": "council-of-pisa-milan", "sourceName": "Council of Pisa-Milan", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "rené-de-prie", "targetName": "René de Prie", "context": "The Council of Pisa-Milan profoundly influenced René de Prie's career, leading to his excommunication and subsequent political maneuvering for reconciliation."}], "places": [{"name": "Angers, France", "role": "Bishopric/Power Base"}, {"name": "Pisa, Italy", "role": "Council Location"}, {"name": "Rome, Italy", "role": "Papal Curia"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: René de Prie", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "rené-de-prie"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1389747", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ren%C3%A9_de_Prie"], "tags": ["France", "Religious Figures", "Europe", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "René de Prie's prominent role in the schismatic Council of Pisa-Milan demonstrated the intense power struggles between the French monarchy and the Papacy, briefly contributing to a church schism and highlighting the limits of papal authority in the early 16th century.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-11T05:15:03Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29140695830", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "René de Prie (1451–1519) was a French Roman Catholic bishop and cardinal.", "newValue": "René de Prie (1451–1519) emerged as a pivotal, if controversial, figure in early 16th-century French ecclesiastical politics, serving as a Roman Catholic cardinal and bishop whose career was deeply intertwined with the shifting alliances and power struggles between the French monarchy and the Papacy during the Italian Wars. Born into the influential Prie family, he was appointed Bishop of Bayeux in 1498 and later elevated to the cardinalate by Pope Julius II in 1506, a strategic move by the pontiff to secure French support, which ultimately proved fragile.

De Prie's most notable, and indeed infamous, involvement came with his prominent role in the schismatic Council of Pisa (1511) and later Milan, convened by King Louis XII of France in opposition to Pope Julius II. As a leading cardinal within this assembly, he actively participated in the deposition of Julius II, an act that led to his own excommunication by the legitimate Pope in October 1511. This bold defiance underscored the profound tensions between secular rulers seeking to control national churches and the universal authority claimed by the Roman pontiff, a conflict that defined much of the late medieval and early modern period.

Following the death of Julius II in 1513 and the ascension of Pope Leo X, René de Prie sought reconciliation with the Holy See. He was absolved of his excommunication and restored to his cardinalatial dignity in 1513, demonstrating the Papacy's pragmatic need to mend fences with powerful French figures. His career thus serves as a microcosm of the intricate diplomatic maneuvering and the often-blurred lines between political expediency and religious loyalty that characterized the era, leaving a legacy of a church grappling with internal dissent and external pressures from burgeoning nation-states."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-11T05:15:03Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29140695830", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1451, Touraine"}, {"title": "Spiritual seeking and religious community formation", "type": "Idea", "year": "1451, Touraine"}], "newValue": ["The political ambitions of King Louis XII of France to assert control over the French church and challenge papal authority created an environment for figures like De Prie to thrive.", "The ongoing Italian Wars (1494–1559) led to shifting alliances between European powers and the Papacy, necessitating ecclesiastical figures who could serve as diplomatic proxies.", "The lingering influence of conciliarist ideas from the Great Western Schism encouraged some churchmen to challenge papal supremacy through general councils."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-11T05:15:03Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29140695830", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped religious practice and spiritual communities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1519-09-19"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1519-09-19"}], "newValue": ["René de Prie's participation in the Counc